WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.167
-Our first guest
is a four-time Oscar nominee

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:05.901
starring in the acclaimed
new film "Nightmare Alley,"

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.601
which is in theaters now,
and also in the giant --

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:10.200
probably the biggest movie of
all time,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.067
"Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:15.033
Here's Willem Dafoe!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.501
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:18.667
That's exactly right.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.501
Hey, buddy.
-Hey, Jimmy.

00:00:20.501 --> 00:00:22.234
-It's great to see you, buddy.
-How are you?

00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:23.334
-Thank you so much
for being on the show.

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:25.834
-Good to see you.
Sure, my pleasure.

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:28.467
-That's a beautiful door
behind you.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.767
-Yeah, it's nice, huh?
-I love it, yeah.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:35.901
You might be the first person
to Zoom from Rome on our show,

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:37.801
so thank you for doing that.
-Ah, okay.

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.434
-I appreciate that.
-Yeah, my pleasure.

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:42.167
-Yeah.
Do you live in Rome?

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:44.367
-I live in Rome,
and I also live in New York.

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:47.067
I married an Italian,
so I live both places.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.734
-Yeah.
What is your favorite pasta?

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:51.067
Do you have a favorite pasta?

00:00:51.067 --> 00:00:53.033
-You know,
I like the simplest kind.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.901
Spaghetti aglio e olio
e peperoncino.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.400
Very simple.
-That's my man.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.601
Yes, and peperoncino.
That is so good.

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.701
All right, I want to come
stay at your place.

00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:03.734
-That would be my last meal.

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.400
People ask me
that question a lot.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.667
"What would be your last meal?"
That would be it.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:08.868
-That would be it?
Wow.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.434
Is there a place
that you get it from?

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:14.033
That you know?
-Where else? My kitchen.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:16.467
[ Laughter ]
-You're a good man.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:18.100
You know, you've had
such a great career.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.400
I was looking at some stuff
on the Internet,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.167
all the stuff about you,
and you've been in

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.167
over 100 movies.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.767
And "The New York Times,"
they named you

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.968
one of the 25 greatest actors
of the 21st century.

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:34.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:38.601
Not too shabby.
-It's fantastic.

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:39.934
-Not too shabby.
-No, it's nice.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:44.767
I --
Hometown paper, you know?

00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:47.467
When "New York Times"
says something nice about you,

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:50.267
you do pay attention.
-Yeah, you do, I agree.

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:52.467
I've been on
the opposite side of that.

00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:54.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.400
What do you think?
What do you think

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.801
when you see your name
on that list?

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:59.968
And you go, "Well, yeah."
You're like a young kid going,

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:01.300
"I might try acting."

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.734
Now you're one of
the greatest actors ever.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:05.901
-You know, it's encouragement.
What can you say?

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.701
-Yeah.
-It just -- You know.

00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:09.901
Things are sometimes
a little uncertain,

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:13.567
you know, being an actor.
And it just gives you energy

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:16.767
and tells you to keep on going,
and lets you know

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:19.200
someone's out there watching.
-Yeah.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.901
A lot of people are out there
watching you, buddy.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:25.467
I also saw you got a Hollywood
star on the Walk of Fame,

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.501
which I assume that you
should've gotten already.

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:31.000
But that's kind of awesome.
That's kind of cool, right?

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.901
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah, I mean, it's --

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:37.868
I remember when,
first time I went to L.A.,

00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:40.734
I'm bringing my kid there,
and he loved movies.

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:43.300
And I would take him
and show him the stars

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.367
on the Walk of Fame.

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.934
And then,
to be part of that is --

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:50.167
Yeah, it's -- it's not something
I was thinking about,

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:54.033
but it's -- I'm very happy that
they invited me there, so...

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:56.801
-Do you know who -- what other
stars are next to you?

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:58.167
'Cause that would be
concerning for me.

00:02:58.167 --> 00:02:59.467
I would...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:01.133
-There goes the neighborhood,
right?

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:03.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:05.534
-That's why they don't
want to give me the star,

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:07.968
'cause it'd just bum
everyone else out.

00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:09.868
Let's talk about your movies.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:12.033
First of all, "Nightmare Alley."

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:16.067
Wow, is it a great film
and just...

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:18.133
"creepy" is probably
the best way I can put it.

00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:21.534
-This is very much
kind of a new noir.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.601
-Yes, it is noir.
-It's kind of a rethinking

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:30.400
of the classic --
classic film of the same title.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.801
I play the head of a carnival.

00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:34.667
I run a geek show.
-Yep.

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:38.033
-Kind of a dark figure.
But a fun figure to play.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.601
Pragmatic.
Takes care of his people.

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:43.300
Knows who's he is,
but maybe not --

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.701
You don't want him
as your neighbor.

00:03:44.701 --> 00:03:47.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:50.033
-Hence the Hollywood Walk of --
the Hollywood star.

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.834
You have your whole block
to yourself.

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:56.000
And how's Bradley Cooper?
Is he doing okay?

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.934
It's our old buddy.
-He's a good egg.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:00.133
I mean,
he's got a good work ethic,

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:02.601
and he lives
and breathes movies.

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:04.367
He loves movies.
-Yeah, he does.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:07.467
-They had shot
half of the movie before --

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:09.200
they had to take a break
for COVID.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.400
-Yep.
-And then, by time I came in,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.701
I'm in the first half
of the movie,

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:17.701
the first part of the movie,
and Guillermo and him

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:19.834
had such a shorthand
that is was really fun

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.434
to work with the two of them.

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:24.801
Most of my stuff
is with Bradley.

00:04:24.801 --> 00:04:26.334
You know,
we were inventing stuff.

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:27.701
We were playing around
with stuff.

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.067
Sometimes that stuff
doesn't make it in the movie.

00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.701
But you kind of feel like...
-Yeah.

00:04:31.701 --> 00:04:34.267
-...that informs what is
in the movie

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:38.634
and kind of
sends the scenes on their way.

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:40.334
-Yeah.
He came on our show once,

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:42.934
and we started laughing,
and we probably laughed

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:45.634
for 25 minutes straight.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.901
Couldn't use any of it.
But we just couldn't stop.

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:50.067
-Sounds like
a real fun show, Jimmy!

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:52.334
[ Laughter ]
-Everyone else was bummed, yeah.

00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:56.968
It was like -- yeah.
So, you do this beautiful movie.

00:04:56.968 --> 00:04:59.601
I would say beautiful because it
really is just a gorgeous film.

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:02.400
And then, you do another
well-shot movie

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.367
but more of a pop hit.
You do "Spider-Man."

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:09.434
And it's --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:14.634
It's already made over
like $1 billion or something.

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:17.400
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.367
-In theaters only, though.
Right?

00:05:20.367 --> 00:05:24.334
-Yeah, but so is
"Nightmare Alley" right now.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:27.601
And they're two movies that you
really want to see in theaters.

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:29.968
Just because...

00:05:29.968 --> 00:05:32.701
Well, for "Spider-Man,"
the size of the effects

00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:35.834
and just the size of the story.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:39.167
And also,
for "Nightmare Alley" --

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:41.467
Well, they're both
beautifully shot.

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:42.868
They're movies
you should see in the theater.

00:05:42.868 --> 00:05:45.334
So, right now, they're both
in theaters, so...

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:46.701
-Yeah.
-...run out.

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:49.133
-Go out and check them out.
Wear a mask and enjoy.

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:53.100
Yeah -- Did you --
Was it so secretive?

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.634
'Cause I didn't -- I had no idea
you were in this movie.

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:58.801
I know you knew
that you were in it, but...

00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:00.234
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:03.467
Did they, like, hide it?
Did they have you wear a --

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:06.267
-They did, they did.
And they did it beautifully.

00:06:06.267 --> 00:06:09.267
And, first of all, I thought,
you know, these movies --

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.767
these Marvel movies,
have such a strong fan base.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:13.167
People are so curious
about them.

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:14.367
Even when they're being made,

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:17.834
people are trying to figure out
what's going on.

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:20.601
But they really decided
they were going to keep

00:06:20.601 --> 00:06:22.801
some of the elements a secret.

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:24.767
And I won't say what
some of those elements are

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:26.601
for anyone
that hasn't seen the movie.

00:06:26.601 --> 00:06:30.801
But, for me --
Yeah, when I'd go to set,

00:06:30.801 --> 00:06:34.133
they put me in a black cloak
and always had me in a car

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:36.234
with dark windows.

00:06:36.234 --> 00:06:38.567
And they didn't want me
hanging out anyplace

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:39.834
because they didn't want --

00:06:39.834 --> 00:06:42.767
-Didn't want to know
that you were in town.

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:44.901
-And I didn't think
it would work,

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.801
but, actually, it did,
and only just before

00:06:46.801 --> 00:06:49.934
the movie was released,
they started

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:52.467
kind of hinting things
in the trailers.

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:54.601
So it was --
-They did a great job.

00:06:54.601 --> 00:06:56.100
-It was cool.
-Yeah, it was cool.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.634
And you're amazing in it.

00:06:57.634 --> 00:06:59.434
And thank you again for coming
on the show. I appreciate it.

00:06:59.434 --> 00:07:00.701
I want to show everyone a clip.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.701 --> 00:07:02.834
-My pleasure.
-Here's Willem Dafoe

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:07.834
in "Spider-Man: No Way Home."
Take a look at this.

00:07:07.834 --> 00:07:09.834
-Octavius?

00:07:13.067 --> 00:07:15.167
-Osborn?

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:16.701
-What --
What happened to you?

00:07:16.701 --> 00:07:19.267
-What happened to --

00:07:19.267 --> 00:07:21.234
You're the walking corpse.

00:07:21.234 --> 00:07:25.234
-What do you mean?
-You died, Norman.

00:07:25.234 --> 00:07:27.167
Years ago.

00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:30.501
-You're insane.
-God I love it, here.

00:07:30.501 --> 00:07:32.467
-What are you talking about?

00:07:32.467 --> 00:07:33.534
He's standing right there.
He's not --

00:07:33.534 --> 00:07:35.734
-Dead.

00:07:35.734 --> 00:07:39.901
They both died,
fighting Spider-Man.

00:07:39.901 --> 00:07:42.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:42.634 --> 00:07:45.567
-What?!
So cool!

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:48.334
Willem Dafoe, everybody.
"Spider-Man: No Way Home"

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:50.133
and "Nightmare Alley"
are in theaters.

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:52.133
Go to the theaters
and check them out.

